// Example: a harder decade for money.
//
// Every weight the monetary model has is a row in `common/inflation`, and this file names
// only the ones it disagrees with. Everything unnamed keeps the base game's value, so this
// mod does not go stale the next time something else in that file is retuned.
//
// What it does: prices are harder to hold down, the bank is more hawkish and moves faster,
// and a government that takes the rate off the bank pays a great deal more for cheap money.
// The result is a world where monetary policy is a live decision every year rather than a
// slider most players never touch.
{
  // Where a settled country rests. Base game 0.018. Four per cent is the 1970s rather than
  // the 2010s, and every effect below is measured from `comfort`, so raising this alone
  // makes a calm world feel permanently slightly wrong - which is the point.
  "base": 0.040,

  // What a deficit does. The largest single thing a player does to their own prices, and
  // raising it is the cleanest way to make borrowing feel like a decision.
  "deficit": 0.80,

  // How fast a country gets where it is heading, per economic week. Base game 0.10, so about
  // ten weeks to close most of a gap; 0.18 makes a budget felt within a season.
  "approach": 0.18,

  "central_bank": {
    // What the line's neutral point means, per annum. The whole key-rate scale is read
    // against this, so raising it moves every rate in the game: the minimum the budget line
    // allows becomes 1.4 % rather than 0.8 %, and the maximum 21 % rather than 12 %.
    "neutral_rate": 0.07,

    // What the bank is aiming at. Above the base game's two per cent, because a bank that
    // aims at two in a world that rests at four spends the whole game tightening.
    "target": 0.03,

    // Points of rate per point of overshoot. Above one in any sane model - a bank that
    // raises by less than prices rose has loosened while appearing to tighten - and 2.2 is
    // a bank with something to prove.
    "response": 2.2,

    // How much of the distance it covers in an economic week. 0.45 is a bank that moves in
    // big steps and surprises people.
    "smoothing": 0.45,

    // How much overshoot it forgives during a war. Zero is a bank that does not care that
    // there is a war on, which is a genuinely different world to play in.
    "war_tolerance": 0.004,

    // And what a government pays, per point it holds the rate below what the bank wanted.
    // At 0.9 a government holding the rate four points under the bank adds three and a half
    // points to inflation every week it lasts - cheap money becomes a decision with a date
    // attached rather than a free win.
    "credibility": 0.90,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- the printing press --
    //
    // And the other lever a seized bank unlocks. This decade is one where printing is
    // possible, tempting and ruinous - which is a different game to one where it is merely
    // expensive.

    // How much of a year's output may be printed in a year. Base game 0.15. A quarter is a
    // world where a desperate government really can print its way through one more year.
    "emission_ceiling": 0.25,

    // Points of inflation per unit printed, directly. Base game 0.90.
    "emission_inflation": 1.10,

    // And what it costs in belief. Base game 1.60; at 2.4 a country printing a tenth of its
    // output is adding a quarter of a point to prices every week through confidence alone,
    // on top of the money itself.
    "emission_credibility": 2.40,

    // How fast that is forgiven once the press stops. Base game 0.12, about half a year.
    // At 0.05 it is nearer two years, so a war paid for with the press is a decade of
    // prices rather than a bad season.
    "emission_memory_decay": 0.05
  }
}
